  "summary": "Nine items, mostly published, apply scRNA-seq or snRNA-seq to immune and epithelial compartments in sepsis, malaria, GPA, chronic rhinosinusitis, lung injury,…",
  "synthesis": "The activity centers on single-cell transcriptomic profiling of immune compartments, with monocyte and neutrophil heterogeneity recurring across items. A single-cell atlas integrating 1 million human neutrophil transcriptomes from 781 samples and 43 datasets maps subsets across health and disease. In sepsis, S100A8 marks an emergency-myeloid, HLA-DR-low monocyte state, which the item notes is not a validated diagnostic or therapeutic. Pediatric malaria scRNA-seq reports reduced monocyte-T cell interactions and inflammatory S100A8/S100A9 monocytes. GPA neutrophil analysis identifies seven clusters, with PLA2G7-high cells linked to relapse and renal involvement. Further items cover macrophage endotypes in chronic rhinosinusitis, porcine Klebsiella lung atlases, alveolar transitional states after lung injury, and nanoplastic-exposed monocytes. The 3.2 rise ratio reflects item counts (7 published, 2 preprints); the items do not establish drivers of this increase.",
